  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nGlobal and synoptic models have begun leaning towards a bit \nquicker frontal passage with the cold front on Tuesday as a\nshortwave over Canada will matriculate east, pulling a cold \nfront through the Midwest and into Missouri. Despite this, \ntemperatures remain expected to again climb into the 90s on \nTuesday with additional moisture, allowing heat index values to \nonce again climb into the 100 to 110 range. \n\nSome models continue to show the cold air behind the front\nlagging behind the front/wind shift by several hours. The level\nto which the temperatures can climb will depend on the time the\nfront moves through the area, which currently looks to have \nmoves earlier into the late morning or around the midday period,\nand when the cooler air makes it into the region. \n\nThus, it may be possible for some areas across extreme \nsouthwest Missouri and southeast Kansas could see heat indices \napproaching or even slightly above 110 degrees (warning \ncriteria) though the potential for this is on the low side. So, \na Heat Advisory remains for the entire area from 1 PM today \nthrough 7 PM Tuesday for afternoon heat indices between 100 and \n110 degrees. The NWS HeatRisk product likewise depicts \nwidespread Moderate to Major values, suggesting those without \neffective cooling and hydration measures are likely to face \nheat-related impacts.\n\nIn addition to the heat, but to a lesser degree, will be the \npotential for shower an storm development Tuesday afternoon as \nthe front moves through. The latest models continue to have\nissues with where, when and if convection develops along this \nboundary. However, confidence is low with that the region will \nsee and strong to severe storms are even extensive coverage. If \nconvection does initiate, RAP 0-3 km theta-e differences around \n25-30 deg C may support a few stronger downdrafts and wind \ngusts.\n\nBehind the cold frontal passage, drier and cooler airmass will \nmove in into Wednesday, which will act to keep rain chances \nminimal through Thursday. Forecast highs range from the low 80s \nacross the eastern Ozarks to low 90s across extreme southwest \nMissouri and southeast Kansas, and with dew points around 60 \ndegrees, conditions will feel much more pleasant. \n\nGlobal models then begin to flatten the ridge as another shortwave\ntrough moves through the area late Thursday which brings the\nnext chances for rain into Friday morning. There will remain the\npotential for rain chances into the weekend as the region\nremains under the influence of northwesterly flow aloft into \nearly next week.",
